1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,760
One of the deadliest natural disasters in United States

2
00:00:05,761 --> 00:00:08,640
history.

3
00:00:08,641 --> 00:00:12,000
Hurricane Katrina devastated New Orleans,

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,520
submerging it under a torrent of flood water.

5
00:00:15,521 --> 00:00:19,000
Levees failed, and water pumping systems below sea level

6
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:21,200
were flooded.

7
00:00:21,201 --> 00:00:24,240
The roof of the Superdome, where thousands sought shelter,

8
00:00:24,241 --> 00:00:27,280
was torn to shreds.

9
00:00:27,281 --> 00:00:32,920
And this bridge, linking the city to outside relief efforts, collapsed like a set of dominoes.

10
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:35,380
How could it all happen?

11
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:40,380
Now, Engineering Disasters, New Orleans, on Modern Marvels.

12
00:00:53,369 --> 00:01:00,740
In August 2005, the close to half million residents of New Orleans knew all hell was about to break loose.

13
00:01:02,180 --> 00:01:08,900
I urge all citizens to put their own safety and the safety of their families first by moving to safe ground.

14
00:01:10,380 --> 00:01:17,120
A monster named Hurricane Katrina was tearing across the Gulf of Mexico.

15
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:23,240
The Category 5 hurricane, backing winds of nearly 175 miles per hour,

16
00:01:23,580 --> 00:01:28,300
was bearing down on the city and was predicted to make landfall after dark.

17
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:35,700
As expected, Katrina pounded the Louisiana coastline.

18
00:01:35,701 --> 00:01:46,260
However, after it made landfall, it was downgraded to a Category 4 storm with winds of less than 150 miles per hour.

19
00:01:47,540 --> 00:01:51,880
And the eye of the hurricane veered northeast, away from the city.

20
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:57,460
At the last second, it made this little turn, and we're all so excited.

21
00:01:57,540 --> 00:01:59,500
It's like, no, we're not going to get the brunt of the storm.

22
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:01,220
New Orleans is going to make it.

23
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:02,160
We're going to make it.

24
00:02:02,161 --> 00:02:06,560
Celebrations would prove to be short-lived.

25
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:11,980
By missing the city, Katrina's ferocious winds,

26
00:02:12,400 --> 00:02:15,620
moving in a counterclockwise pattern over Lake Pontchartrain to the north

27
00:02:15,621 --> 00:02:18,340
and the Gulf of Mexico to the east,

28
00:02:19,120 --> 00:02:22,000
sent an estimated 15-foot storm surge into the city

29
00:02:22,001 --> 00:02:24,340
through its backdoor drainage canal system.

30
00:02:24,341 --> 00:02:33,480
Storm surge is the swell of water caused by the hurricane winds.

31
00:02:33,481 --> 00:02:44,380
And it raises the water level, so it's as though it's a high tide that is enormously high and just comes in fast.

32
00:02:46,940 --> 00:02:51,220
Only the levees and flood walls could protect the city from the impending catastrophe.

33
00:02:54,340 --> 00:03:00,200
But in a series of engineering disasters, the levees failed on a massive scale.

34
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:10,700
Once the breach occurred, there was a wall of water released, like a tsunami, that flowed into this residential area.

35
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:18,900
First, a levee flood wall broke along the Industrial Canal waterway on the city's east side.

36
00:03:18,901 --> 00:03:28,580
Storm surge water rushed through the breach into an area known as the Ninth Ward.

37
00:03:30,140 --> 00:03:33,800
I heard a loud boom, explosion like boom.

38
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:40,800
The water was so powerful that I know something was wrong because

39
00:03:40,801 --> 00:03:44,700
there ain't no way in the world that that much pressure of water is supposed to be coming.

40
00:03:48,900 --> 00:03:53,140
Then a second flood wall on top of the 17th Street Canal levy

41
00:03:53,141 --> 00:03:55,660
suffered a two-city block-wide breach.

42
00:03:57,940 --> 00:04:00,140
This allowed the water of Lake Pontchartrain

43
00:04:00,141 --> 00:04:01,620
to run into the city.

44
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:06,400
Then a third levy flood wall collapsed

45
00:04:06,401 --> 00:04:08,060
along the London Avenue Canal.

46
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:11,080
You can clearly see how the walls come down

47
00:04:11,081 --> 00:04:13,520
and the water just kind of pushed over it.

48
00:04:13,521 --> 00:04:21,980
These levees and floodwall breaks put more than 80% of Orleans' parish underwater.

49
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:26,200
Thousands of victims were left stranded.

50
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:36,180
We are dealing with one of the worst natural disasters in our nation's history.

51
00:04:36,181 --> 00:04:44,560
With the city submerged, New Orleans' system of mammoth water pumps, used for more than

52
00:04:44,561 --> 00:04:49,560
80 years to drain the city, was of little use.

53
00:04:49,561 --> 00:04:51,600
Our biggest problem was water.

54
00:04:51,601 --> 00:04:55,420
Water rolled into the station, rolled across the floor.

55
00:04:55,421 --> 00:05:00,620
The power had to be cut to them.

56
00:05:00,621 --> 00:05:02,960
Water rose to this height right here.

57
00:05:02,961 --> 00:05:03,960
That's it.

58
00:05:03,961 --> 00:05:04,960
Our pumps are shut down.

59
00:05:04,961 --> 00:05:05,960
We're dead in the water.

60
00:05:05,961 --> 00:05:07,460
decommissioned, no more pumping.

61
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:12,860
But the flooding wasn't the only disaster Katrina visited upon New Orleans.

62
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:19,680
Despite evacuations, up to 25,000 residents remained in the city.

63
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:27,200
Many fled to the Louisiana Superdome, a structure built on higher ground

64
00:05:27,201 --> 00:05:30,880
and supposedly engineered to withstand hurricane-force winds.

65
00:05:30,881 --> 00:05:37,440
however most of its roof was ripped apart during the storm

66
00:05:37,441 --> 00:05:47,500
it was quite devastating to the dome itself it was a horrible environment to be in to be in a space

67
00:05:47,501 --> 00:05:54,440
with a number of holes in the roof water entering uh no electricity the plumbing systems beginning

68
00:05:54,441 --> 00:06:01,200
to back up, all as a result of these tremendous wind pressures on the roof structure itself.

69
00:06:03,340 --> 00:06:07,340
Thousands were rescued from high water, many from rooftops.

70
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:14,820
Getting relief to victims became extraordinarily difficult.

71
00:06:16,740 --> 00:06:22,700
In another engineering disaster, a main route into the city, the Interstate 10 Twin Span

72
00:06:22,701 --> 00:06:27,180
Bridge collapsed as a result of the storm's massive water surge.

73
00:06:28,920 --> 00:06:31,991
This is what the bridge looked like before Katrina.

74
00:06:31,992 --> 00:06:34,280
When this powerful storm came through,

75
00:06:34,460 --> 00:06:36,042
it just devastated the bridge.

76
00:06:36,043 --> 00:06:39,260
And you can see right here that there's nothing left of these

77
00:06:39,261 --> 00:06:46,720
265-ton concrete segments. The city was left with no power.

78
00:06:46,721 --> 00:06:53,200
scarce drinking water, dwindling food supplies, and incidents of looting.

79
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:59,540
Up to 1,300 victims did not survive.

80
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:03,700
New Orleans looked like a third world country.

81
00:07:05,600 --> 00:07:12,620
It was really an apocalyptic event for the city to flood and fires to break out in the water

82
00:07:12,621 --> 00:07:15,260
and people to be trapped in their homes.

83
00:07:15,261 --> 00:07:19,280
the collapse of civilization seeming to be imminent.

84
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:26,000
Why had New Orleans been so vulnerable?

85
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:30,340
A shocked nation began to look for answers.

86
00:07:31,260 --> 00:07:33,000
The mystery that we're trying to uncover

87
00:07:33,001 --> 00:07:35,700
in terms of the engineering performance of the design

88
00:07:35,701 --> 00:07:38,920
is what caused the variety of failures that we saw.

89
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:41,680
Was it a strong hurricane or a weak design?

90
00:07:41,681 --> 00:07:50,220
An army of scientists, geologists, and engineers descended on the crippled city to unlock this mystery.

91
00:07:52,460 --> 00:07:57,300
One of the so-called geodetectives was Dr. Joseph Suheida,

92
00:07:58,120 --> 00:08:04,540
an oceanographer, geotechnical coastal engineer, and former professor at Louisiana State University.

93
00:08:04,541 --> 00:08:14,560
Right after the storm hit, I investigated the causes for some of the breaches and floodwall failures that occurred.

94
00:08:14,860 --> 00:08:23,449
We had to do this immediately after the storm before any evidence would be erased by either repairs or some natural processes that would obscure what we were trying to

95
00:08:23,450 --> 00:08:24,150
look at.

96
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:27,780
Dr. Suheda reached the scene without a moment to spare.

97
00:08:27,781 --> 00:08:33,600
The Army Corps of Engineers was rapidly repairing the levee and floodwall breaks

98
00:08:33,601 --> 00:08:38,840
to protect against further storms, which could strike during the remainder of what would turn out

99
00:08:38,841 --> 00:08:41,200
to be an extremely active hurricane season.

100
00:08:41,920 --> 00:08:45,640
There were a lot of unknown factors in terms of the levee breaches.

101
00:08:46,360 --> 00:08:53,080
And so we had to look into the factors that would explain why they failed under conditions

102
00:08:53,081 --> 00:08:55,380
that would appear that they should not have failed under.

103
00:08:57,780 --> 00:09:01,760
In fact, the engineering failures may have been over 300 years in the making.

104
00:09:05,060 --> 00:09:08,420
Beginning when the French arrived at the end of the 17th century,

105
00:09:10,060 --> 00:09:15,400
the first settlers found a swampy area they called Le Flottant, the floating land.

106
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:23,340
The soil on which New Orleans was built is naturally very wet, even spongy,

107
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:26,800
and consists of silt carried from the Mississippi River.

108
00:09:27,780 --> 00:09:31,480
The city is mostly surrounded by water.

109
00:09:32,740 --> 00:09:35,460
Only parts of New Orleans are a few feet above sea level.

110
00:09:36,380 --> 00:09:39,900
Most of it is either at or 10 feet below sea level.

111
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:44,360
The topography has been likened to a soup bowl

112
00:09:44,361 --> 00:09:47,380
because the front, the river end is high,

113
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:51,020
or the rim, and the middle is low, like the bottom of a bowl.

114
00:09:53,600 --> 00:09:55,460
Before it was known as the Big Easy,

115
00:09:55,461 --> 00:09:58,320
New Orleans was named the Crescent City.

116
00:10:00,820 --> 00:10:05,460
The French first built on the crescent-shaped high elevation along the Mississippi River.

117
00:10:06,700 --> 00:10:09,440
This is where the famous French Quarter is now located.

118
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:17,080
From the beginning, the French had problems with flooding from the Mississippi River.

119
00:10:18,500 --> 00:10:20,920
So they began to build a system of levees.

120
00:10:20,921 --> 00:10:26,740
A levee is an earthen dam that runs along a waterway.

121
00:10:27,740 --> 00:10:31,040
The levee system goes literally to the founding of New Orleans.

122
00:10:31,380 --> 00:10:38,100
I would say the very, very latest, 1720, and possibly 1718.

123
00:10:39,560 --> 00:10:45,180
After the Louisiana Purchase in 1803, the United States took control of the city.

124
00:10:45,181 --> 00:10:52,180
As it grew, the swampland below sea level was converted into farmland.

125
00:10:52,181 --> 00:10:59,780
During the next hundred years, some 350 miles of levees were built to protect this new land.

126
00:10:59,781 --> 00:11:06,580
Individual landowners, plantation owners, were responsible for building and maintaining the levees.

127
00:11:06,581 --> 00:11:11,480
So it was a very private-based, non-governmental type of action.

128
00:11:11,481 --> 00:11:16,760
The dirt that comprises the base of the levees is that soft,

129
00:11:16,761 --> 00:11:18,620
spongy Mississippi Delta soil.

130
00:11:18,621 --> 00:11:24,000
What we have here is an example of a Delta soil in terms of its

131
00:11:24,001 --> 00:11:27,360
characteristics of having high organic content, being very,

132
00:11:27,361 --> 00:11:31,360
very weak, having high clay content.

133
00:11:31,361 --> 00:11:37,580
In 1927, the United States Army Corps of Engineers took

134
00:11:37,581 --> 00:11:38,900
control of the levee system.

135
00:11:39,860 --> 00:11:42,680
The Corps widened many of the levees and built them higher.

136
00:11:43,240 --> 00:11:46,300
But as the system was strengthened, there was another problem.

137
00:11:46,760 --> 00:11:52,140
New Orleans and the metropolitan area is sinking because of the geology of the soils beneath the city.

138
00:11:52,360 --> 00:11:54,920
The water that's in the material is being squeezed out,

139
00:11:55,220 --> 00:11:59,460
and so the surface has been moving down and will continue to move down for hundreds of years.

140
00:12:00,720 --> 00:12:02,860
This sinking is known as subsidence.

141
00:12:04,440 --> 00:12:06,720
It's caused by the reclaiming of swampland.

142
00:12:07,640 --> 00:12:09,580
When water and mud were pumped out,

143
00:12:10,260 --> 00:12:12,980
the area began to fall even further below sea level.

144
00:12:15,220 --> 00:12:17,960
Subsidance at one time had been balanced by sedimentation.

145
00:12:19,240 --> 00:12:23,100
New silt, sand, and clay deposited when the Mississippi River flooded.

146
00:12:26,480 --> 00:12:28,260
But since the levees were constructed,

147
00:12:28,261 --> 00:12:30,040
the river has been controlled,

148
00:12:30,720 --> 00:12:32,580
and sedimentation has stopped in New Orleans.

149
00:12:33,560 --> 00:12:39,520
The estimates vary for the sinking rates, but we have them as high as one inch a year,

150
00:12:40,060 --> 00:12:45,620
the typical being about a half an inch a year of actual sinking of the surface downward over time.

151
00:12:46,640 --> 00:12:53,700
Even at a rate of a half inch per year, the city will subside or sink more than four feet in a century.

152
00:12:55,060 --> 00:12:59,800
For decades, geological experts warned of the peril of this sinking city

153
00:12:59,801 --> 00:13:02,540
and the fragile levee system that protected it.

154
00:13:03,740 --> 00:13:05,940
This was a disaster waiting to happen

155
00:13:05,941 --> 00:13:08,100
because we knew there were hurricanes

156
00:13:08,101 --> 00:13:10,680
that could flood the city with the protection that we had.

157
00:13:12,520 --> 00:13:15,100
Dr. Suheita was determined to find the failures

158
00:13:15,101 --> 00:13:16,500
that caused the levees to break.

159
00:13:17,220 --> 00:13:19,280
The evidence he was about to uncover

160
00:13:19,281 --> 00:13:21,340
would provide the first clues

161
00:13:21,341 --> 00:13:24,380
to how this engineering disaster could have occurred.

162
00:13:29,800 --> 00:13:34,520
Weeks after Katrina wreaked havoc on New Orleans,

163
00:13:34,980 --> 00:13:36,780
the city remained mostly underwater.

164
00:13:40,580 --> 00:13:43,940
The critical question, why did the levees fail?

165
00:13:45,140 --> 00:13:47,940
Did Katrina overwhelm the city's flood defenses,

166
00:13:48,520 --> 00:13:51,320
or did faulty construction cause them to burst open?

167
00:13:52,780 --> 00:13:56,180
The levees and flood walls were built to what we refer to simply

168
00:13:56,181 --> 00:13:58,240
as a Category 3 fast-moving storm.

169
00:13:59,800 --> 00:14:06,800
The National Hurricane Center classifies hurricanes according to wind speed and storm surge.

170
00:14:06,801 --> 00:14:14,800
The scale ranges from the least severe Category 1 hurricane, with winds of 74 to 95 miles per

171
00:14:14,801 --> 00:14:21,800
hour and a storm surge of 4 to 5 feet, to the most destructive Category 5, where winds

172
00:14:21,801 --> 00:14:28,800
soar to more than 155 miles per hour, with a storm surge of higher than 18 feet.

173
00:14:28,801 --> 00:14:34,840
Well, we started the Hurricane Protection Project back after Hurricane Betsy in 1965.

174
00:14:37,520 --> 00:14:40,580
Katrina wasn't the first hurricane to devastate New Orleans.

175
00:14:42,840 --> 00:14:50,320
In 1965, Hurricane Betsy, a Category 4 storm with winds of 125 miles per hour,

176
00:14:51,020 --> 00:14:55,400
hit New Orleans dead on and plowed through the outdated dirt levee system,

177
00:14:55,401 --> 00:14:59,520
flooding the Ninth Ward and killing 75 people.

178
00:15:02,140 --> 00:15:04,660
Although Betsy caused less damage than Katrina,

179
00:15:05,380 --> 00:15:08,300
there was an effort to ensure that this would not happen again.

180
00:15:10,920 --> 00:15:13,740
The U.S. Congress gave the Army Corps of Engineers

181
00:15:13,741 --> 00:15:16,320
the responsibility of building concrete flood walls

182
00:15:16,321 --> 00:15:20,440
on top of the 7-foot-high levees to give the city more protection.

183
00:15:20,441 --> 00:15:25,680
They're known as eye walls.

184
00:15:26,800 --> 00:15:32,720
One-inch thick steel sheet pilings were driven 20 to 25 feet underground to secure the base.

185
00:15:33,680 --> 00:15:39,680
Tied to them, reinforced concrete walls six inches thick were built on top of the dirt levees.

186
00:15:42,020 --> 00:15:46,720
However, the Army Corps constructed only eight-foot-high flood walls on top of the levees

187
00:15:46,721 --> 00:15:50,640
for an average total of about 15 feet of protection.

188
00:15:52,140 --> 00:15:55,900
These could contain only a Category 3 hurricane storm surge.

189
00:15:59,320 --> 00:16:02,980
To fully protect the city against a Category 5 storm surge,

190
00:16:03,660 --> 00:16:06,200
critics warned higher flood walls should be constructed.

191
00:16:07,200 --> 00:16:09,580
The Army Corps of Engineers in New Orleans was only authorized

192
00:16:09,581 --> 00:16:12,600
to build a Category 3 protection-type system

193
00:16:12,601 --> 00:16:14,340
by the Congress of the United States.

194
00:16:14,341 --> 00:16:17,260
a Category 5 would have required different authorizations

195
00:16:17,261 --> 00:16:19,920
and an extremely high amount more of funding.

196
00:16:22,860 --> 00:16:25,300
Katrina was a Category 4 storm,

197
00:16:25,880 --> 00:16:28,440
with an estimated surge of more than 15 feet.

198
00:16:31,100 --> 00:16:34,460
Geo Detective Joseph Suheida uncovered evidence

199
00:16:34,461 --> 00:16:36,740
suggesting one flood wall failure was caused

200
00:16:36,741 --> 00:16:38,740
by the action of the water overtopping it.

201
00:16:41,100 --> 00:16:43,220
This occurred on the east side of the city,

202
00:16:43,221 --> 00:16:44,940
at the Industrial Canal.

203
00:16:46,200 --> 00:16:48,280
It was in a condition like this

204
00:16:48,281 --> 00:16:50,300
where the water would come over the top of the flood wall

205
00:16:50,301 --> 00:16:53,320
and actually plunge down and scour at the base.

206
00:16:53,700 --> 00:16:56,160
And here below me, you can see what is remaining

207
00:16:56,161 --> 00:16:59,980
of the pit that was scoured out by the flowing water.

208
00:16:59,981 --> 00:17:02,340
And that scouring action would continue

209
00:17:02,341 --> 00:17:04,720
as long as the water kept flowing

210
00:17:04,721 --> 00:17:05,920
over the top of the flood wall.

211
00:17:07,560 --> 00:17:10,500
Scouring occurs when flood water pours over a wall,

212
00:17:10,740 --> 00:17:11,520
like a waterfall.

213
00:17:13,220 --> 00:17:20,320
The force of the falling water from the top of the flood wall scours, or digs out, the

214
00:17:20,321 --> 00:17:26,280
dirt along the wall's foundation, undermining it and causing the entire wall to collapse

215
00:17:26,281 --> 00:17:28,460
from top to bottom.

216
00:17:28,461 --> 00:17:31,460
After the breach occurred, there was a wall of water released.

217
00:17:31,461 --> 00:17:35,540
It would be hundreds of thousands or millions of pounds of force.

218
00:17:35,541 --> 00:17:38,100
The wall of water was unbelievable.

219
00:17:38,101 --> 00:17:41,460
It had calls and everything, boasting down.

220
00:17:41,461 --> 00:17:46,500
Everything in his past, cars and everything popping up from the surplates and everything.

221
00:17:46,980 --> 00:17:49,000
And I've never seen nothing like that in my life.

222
00:17:49,460 --> 00:17:50,160
It was unbelievable.

223
00:17:51,380 --> 00:17:57,520
Ninth Ward resident Palazzolo Simmons lived just a few blocks from the Industrial Canal levee when it failed.

224
00:17:58,860 --> 00:18:00,720
His home was totally destroyed.

225
00:18:01,480 --> 00:18:05,260
I was right inside of here when that storm come through here.

226
00:18:05,620 --> 00:18:07,880
And I could not have been here talking to y'all.

227
00:18:08,820 --> 00:18:09,620
Could have been dead.

228
00:18:11,460 --> 00:18:15,260
The water was so deep I had to swim from one root town to another root town.

229
00:18:15,960 --> 00:18:17,440
I was stranded for three days.

230
00:18:17,980 --> 00:18:20,160
Three days with no rescue help whatsoever.

231
00:18:21,320 --> 00:18:22,860
I got out of this area.

232
00:18:23,520 --> 00:18:27,300
A guy came in with a boat, rowing a boat.

233
00:18:27,900 --> 00:18:30,940
He seen me, and he came and picked me up.

234
00:18:32,800 --> 00:18:34,380
Elizolo Simmons was lucky.

235
00:18:35,100 --> 00:18:35,940
He survived.

236
00:18:36,660 --> 00:18:39,680
In his ninth ward, which was hit hardest by the storm surge,

237
00:18:40,400 --> 00:18:41,800
Miles of homes were destroyed.

238
00:18:45,900 --> 00:18:47,460
In another part of the city,

239
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,680
a more insidious culprit caused the massive failures

240
00:18:50,681 --> 00:18:54,420
on both the London Avenue Canal and the 17th Street Canal,

241
00:18:54,800 --> 00:18:58,600
the very building material of the levees themselves,

242
00:18:59,040 --> 00:19:00,160
the soil.

243
00:19:01,440 --> 00:19:03,720
As we can see here, the flood wall is in place.

244
00:19:03,721 --> 00:19:04,560
It's vertical.

245
00:19:04,840 --> 00:19:06,120
It's got a nice clean edge.

246
00:19:06,200 --> 00:19:07,760
We don't see any of the breaking or bending

247
00:19:07,761 --> 00:19:09,620
that occurred in the industrial canal.

248
00:19:09,680 --> 00:19:12,360
And that's because the mechanism of failure was different.

249
00:19:12,760 --> 00:19:15,620
In this case, the soils that were holding up the flood wall

250
00:19:15,621 --> 00:19:18,880
were saturated by the storm surge, weakened and failed,

251
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:20,380
and then slid and raised up,

252
00:19:20,680 --> 00:19:23,580
pushing that soil mass in the flood walls into the homes.

253
00:19:25,260 --> 00:19:26,900
This is known as heaving.

254
00:19:27,980 --> 00:19:30,420
As Katrina's storm surge filled the canal,

255
00:19:30,940 --> 00:19:34,640
water pressure rose in the soft delta soil underneath the flood walls.

256
00:19:35,740 --> 00:19:39,480
When the rising pressure and moving water overcame the soil's strength,

257
00:19:39,680 --> 00:19:45,540
It suddenly shifted, taking surrounding material and the flood walls with it.

258
00:19:45,800 --> 00:19:48,000
This is an extraordinary situation here.

259
00:19:48,340 --> 00:19:53,100
What we have is a piece of the original soil that was holding up the flood wall

260
00:19:53,101 --> 00:19:56,960
that was about 40 feet to my right, right along the 17th Street Canal.

261
00:19:58,340 --> 00:20:03,100
So this material here was actually alongside the channel at one time.

262
00:20:03,160 --> 00:20:04,660
It got displaced and lifted upward.

263
00:20:04,661 --> 00:20:09,140
This fence was slightly outside the area of the flood wall.

264
00:20:09,141 --> 00:20:13,140
So we've seen a movement and an uplifting of the soils by about 40 feet.

265
00:20:14,640 --> 00:20:19,180
This movement might have been prevented if another engineering failure had not occurred.

266
00:20:22,580 --> 00:20:29,320
The steel sheet pile bases, driven 20 to 25 feet underground, gave the flood walls insufficient support.

267
00:20:30,660 --> 00:20:35,100
A deeper sheet piling would probably have anchored the flood wall in much stronger soil.

268
00:20:35,101 --> 00:20:41,100
If the soil movement takes place below the bottom of that sheet pile,

269
00:20:41,220 --> 00:20:45,400
then the sheet pile is really passive and doesn't protect from the soil motion.

270
00:20:45,820 --> 00:20:47,820
And that's what happened at the 17th Street Canal.

271
00:20:48,300 --> 00:20:52,720
But if the soil is strong enough, then the sheet pile actually holds up the concrete wall.

272
00:20:56,000 --> 00:21:01,240
Along the London Avenue Canal, the Army Corps of Engineers is repairing the floodwall breaches

273
00:21:01,241 --> 00:21:03,640
with deeper steel sheet pilings.

274
00:21:04,640 --> 00:21:07,560
Perhaps they'll prevent a catastrophe of this kind in the future.

275
00:21:08,900 --> 00:21:11,980
These are the old sheet piling underneath the flood wall.

276
00:21:12,560 --> 00:21:16,700
The new sheet piling we're driving are 79 feet, these big ones right here,

277
00:21:17,080 --> 00:21:19,300
and these are the old ones which are 22 feet.

278
00:21:20,100 --> 00:21:24,820
It's a much stronger sheet pile section than the old sheet pile.

279
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:31,980
Another repair the Army Corps has undertaken is the replacement of the old eye walls

280
00:21:31,981 --> 00:21:35,260
with what are known as T-walls.

281
00:21:39,960 --> 00:21:43,700
The floodwall is secured with a concrete base embedded underground.

282
00:21:44,620 --> 00:21:48,540
Attached to the base are steel sheet pilings of up to 70 feet deep.

283
00:21:49,820 --> 00:21:54,760
So you have a lot of capacity in these piles where they can't be moved,

284
00:21:54,860 --> 00:21:58,080
so the wall can't turn as long as those piles are there.

285
00:21:58,081 --> 00:22:03,700
Although the damaged levees are being rebuilt by the Army Corps,

286
00:22:04,080 --> 00:22:07,400
they still will only withstand a Category 3 storm surge.

287
00:22:08,200 --> 00:22:12,400
There are no plans to heighten the flood walls to protect against a Category 5.

288
00:22:12,980 --> 00:22:16,360
The estimates, it would take many, many years, five to ten years at least.

289
00:22:17,220 --> 00:22:22,960
Dollar estimates range in the $20-plus billion range just for New Orleans area.

290
00:22:22,961 --> 00:22:29,220
However, Dr. Joseph Suheita believes even if the flood walls were built higher,

291
00:22:29,740 --> 00:22:32,700
that wouldn't take into account the soil uncertainties,

292
00:22:33,660 --> 00:22:36,500
raising questions about the design of the entire levee system.

293
00:22:39,420 --> 00:22:43,320
The analogy can be made that when you have very soft soils,

294
00:22:43,920 --> 00:22:47,440
even if you have a very strong structure on top of that,

295
00:22:47,880 --> 00:22:50,620
then the whole structure is going to fail,

296
00:22:50,621 --> 00:22:52,900
somewhat like if you put bricks on jello.

297
00:22:53,260 --> 00:22:54,840
The bricks can be very, very strong,

298
00:22:54,980 --> 00:22:56,560
but it's the strength of the jello

299
00:22:56,561 --> 00:22:59,120
that really determines whether the bricks are going to move.

300
00:22:59,840 --> 00:23:01,660
One of the solutions to the problem

301
00:23:01,661 --> 00:23:05,260
of minimizing the threat associated with flood walls

302
00:23:05,261 --> 00:23:08,540
is to build floodgates at the northern end

303
00:23:08,541 --> 00:23:09,800
of our drainage canals,

304
00:23:10,260 --> 00:23:12,420
and therefore keep the storm surge

305
00:23:12,421 --> 00:23:14,140
from coming into the drainage canals

306
00:23:14,141 --> 00:23:16,220
and from threatening the flood walls.

307
00:23:17,680 --> 00:23:19,540
Floodgates are enormous walls

308
00:23:19,541 --> 00:23:22,020
that can be raised or lowered by hydraulic systems

309
00:23:22,021 --> 00:23:23,300
to regulate water flow.

310
00:23:25,620 --> 00:23:28,000
The Army Corps is looking into the possibility

311
00:23:28,001 --> 00:23:30,120
of placing floodgates at canal openings.

312
00:23:32,320 --> 00:23:35,080
The gate would be raised above the level of the water,

313
00:23:35,380 --> 00:23:37,140
so the water would generally pass beneath it.

314
00:23:37,700 --> 00:23:39,380
In the event a storm was approaching,

315
00:23:39,580 --> 00:23:42,920
the gate would be lowered so that it would block the flow of water.

316
00:23:44,640 --> 00:23:47,260
Rebuilt levees, floodwalls, and floodgates

317
00:23:47,261 --> 00:23:50,380
could constitute New Orleans' first line of defense

318
00:23:50,381 --> 00:23:52,220
against another hurricane catastrophe.

319
00:23:54,840 --> 00:23:57,940
That might help protect New Orleans' second line of defense,

320
00:23:58,680 --> 00:24:01,040
a system of 24 water pumping stations

321
00:24:01,041 --> 00:24:02,820
that flooded after Katrina

322
00:24:02,821 --> 00:24:05,740
and left New Orleans crippled for weeks

323
00:24:05,741 --> 00:24:07,960
in a polluted swampland.

324
00:24:07,961 --> 00:24:19,680
For more than two weeks after Katrina hit, New Orleans remained underwater.

325
00:24:24,480 --> 00:24:30,380
The city has one of the world's largest water pumping systems, capable of pumping 30 billion

326
00:24:30,381 --> 00:24:31,442
gallons a day.

327
00:24:31,443 --> 00:24:36,980
But because the levees failed, the pumps, located below sea level, became

328
00:24:36,981 --> 00:24:38,340
inundated with flood water.

329
00:24:41,020 --> 00:24:42,140
Water's coming in.

330
00:24:42,360 --> 00:24:43,240
First time ever.

331
00:24:44,720 --> 00:24:47,700
Electricity and water don't mix.

332
00:24:48,280 --> 00:24:49,860
Operators shut the station down.

333
00:24:49,980 --> 00:24:51,820
Shut the motors off and de-energize.

334
00:24:54,660 --> 00:24:57,200
Not only were the electric pumps immobilized,

335
00:24:58,140 --> 00:25:00,540
pump workers literally had to swim for their lives.

336
00:25:01,380 --> 00:25:04,360
At that point, I had just survival going through my mind.

337
00:25:05,200 --> 00:25:06,120
I wanted to live.

338
00:25:06,980 --> 00:25:07,860
We were all scared.

339
00:25:09,740 --> 00:25:14,100
Pump station attendant Wallace Rainey had to swim in 20-foot deep flood water

340
00:25:14,101 --> 00:25:16,780
to reach a stable place before being rescued.

341
00:25:18,180 --> 00:25:22,500
This is where we stood as the water was rising.

342
00:25:24,720 --> 00:25:26,280
It was coming in through the windows.

343
00:25:26,460 --> 00:25:27,220
The windows were broke.

344
00:25:28,020 --> 00:25:30,500
Had the water coming across the tops of the pumps.

345
00:25:30,640 --> 00:25:31,840
It was lapping at our feet.

346
00:25:32,840 --> 00:25:33,860
We were pretty scared.

347
00:25:33,861 --> 00:25:37,680
We didn't know we were going to make it through that day.

348
00:25:38,900 --> 00:25:41,200
The men who constructed the pump stations

349
00:25:41,201 --> 00:25:43,900
could never have imagined the catastrophic flooding scenario

350
00:25:43,901 --> 00:25:44,900
that followed Katrina.

351
00:25:50,020 --> 00:25:52,620
For centuries, New Orleans had attempted to deal

352
00:25:52,621 --> 00:25:54,300
with water accumulation problems.

353
00:25:55,720 --> 00:25:57,960
But it wasn't until 1912,

354
00:25:58,700 --> 00:26:02,740
when Albert Baldwin Wood invented a successful screw pump

355
00:26:02,741 --> 00:26:04,740
that the city found a solution.

356
00:26:06,620 --> 00:26:09,140
Remarkably, many of Wood's screw pumps,

357
00:26:09,141 --> 00:26:12,020
over 80 years old, are still in use today.

358
00:26:15,840 --> 00:26:17,940
The pumps work on a very simple principle.

359
00:26:19,220 --> 00:26:21,940
Encased in a sealed cast iron drum,

360
00:26:21,941 --> 00:26:25,840
air is transferred out, creating a vacuum inside.

361
00:26:25,841 --> 00:26:28,060
The water is sucked in by the vacuum

362
00:26:28,061 --> 00:26:31,240
and a 14-foot diameter screw or propeller

363
00:26:31,241 --> 00:26:34,380
turning at 83 RPM, propels the water out.

364
00:26:37,260 --> 00:26:39,160
It wasn't the actual pumps,

365
00:26:39,420 --> 00:26:41,380
but rather the electric synchronous motors

366
00:26:41,381 --> 00:26:44,200
which supply the power that were affected by the flooding.

367
00:26:47,100 --> 00:26:49,360
The motors are exposed to the elements

368
00:26:49,361 --> 00:26:52,100
and positioned in pits partially below the floor.

369
00:26:53,580 --> 00:26:56,620
The water got approximately this high on this pump.

370
00:26:56,621 --> 00:27:03,220
And when water hit electricity, boom.

371
00:27:04,500 --> 00:27:06,380
Basically, you have no more operations.

372
00:27:08,520 --> 00:27:13,460
It took weeks for the water to be pumped out of the stations and the motors to thoroughly dry.

373
00:27:16,840 --> 00:27:20,920
Then another problem caused even more delays in getting the pumps back online.

374
00:27:20,921 --> 00:27:24,920
We look at this station and a lot of our other facilities

375
00:27:24,921 --> 00:27:27,720
is working in an actual museum,

376
00:27:27,721 --> 00:27:30,520
because you can look at these pumps and motors,

377
00:27:30,521 --> 00:27:32,520
and they are extremely old.

378
00:27:32,521 --> 00:27:36,720
There's no manufacturer to go back to for these particular parts.

379
00:27:36,721 --> 00:27:41,120
So you're looking at either fabricating them yourself

380
00:27:41,121 --> 00:27:44,520
or going to another fabrication shop to have that done.

381
00:27:46,520 --> 00:27:48,920
The city's sewerage and water board

382
00:27:48,921 --> 00:27:51,480
is preparing its pump system for future flooding.

383
00:27:54,760 --> 00:27:57,160
Already, there are a few prototypes in place

384
00:27:57,161 --> 00:27:59,620
to shield and protect the pump's electrical motors.

385
00:28:02,120 --> 00:28:04,780
After the hurricane, the water was approximately here.

386
00:28:05,200 --> 00:28:06,580
It inundated this pump.

387
00:28:07,140 --> 00:28:11,660
Over at this pump, the water didn't get into the workings of the pump.

388
00:28:11,780 --> 00:28:13,080
It's called a sealed pump.

389
00:28:13,580 --> 00:28:16,660
This pump was able to be started when we got here.

390
00:28:16,661 --> 00:28:24,680
This is a possible solution for a situation, if we ever have it again, that's as traumatic as a storm flooding the city.

391
00:28:29,020 --> 00:28:33,640
Another remedy is to elevate the entire electric system, which runs the pumps.

392
00:28:35,640 --> 00:28:38,040
This has already been accomplished at one station.

393
00:28:39,160 --> 00:28:43,420
Some of this equipment is in the basements of some of these pumping stations.

394
00:28:43,421 --> 00:28:46,060
that equipment possibly needs to be higher

395
00:28:46,061 --> 00:28:49,700
so that if we do get a situation where we need the pumps

396
00:28:49,701 --> 00:28:52,280
and there's a lot of water in the city,

397
00:28:52,620 --> 00:28:54,940
that this equipment stays dry.

398
00:28:56,820 --> 00:29:00,520
It took 21 grueling days to pump out New Orleans.

399
00:29:01,700 --> 00:29:03,600
But if the pumps hadn't been flooded,

400
00:29:04,220 --> 00:29:06,260
would the city itself have stayed above water?

401
00:29:07,120 --> 00:29:10,400
If our drainage pumping station had not been damaged

402
00:29:10,401 --> 00:29:13,400
and the levee breach could be stopped,

403
00:29:13,420 --> 00:29:17,060
in its early stages, it would take 12 to 20 hours

404
00:29:17,061 --> 00:29:20,660
possibly to pump out what ran in during that time.

405
00:29:22,100 --> 00:29:25,180
Instead, standing floodwater left the city

406
00:29:25,181 --> 00:29:26,740
in an uninhabitable soup.

407
00:29:34,740 --> 00:29:37,580
Thousands sought shelter in the New Orleans Superdome.

408
00:29:39,040 --> 00:29:42,800
But another engineering disaster would make this shelter

409
00:29:42,801 --> 00:29:45,380
of last resort, a living nightmare.

410
00:29:52,140 --> 00:29:55,300
With thousands of survivors left stranded in floodwater,

411
00:29:56,280 --> 00:29:59,300
the Louisiana Superdome became a shelter of last resort.

412
00:30:00,980 --> 00:30:02,640
This is a football stadium.

413
00:30:03,420 --> 00:30:04,520
It's not a hospital.

414
00:30:04,920 --> 00:30:05,920
It's not a hotel.

415
00:30:06,440 --> 00:30:09,780
We're equipped to handle people for four hours, not four days.

416
00:30:09,781 --> 00:30:16,540
Conditions deteriorated rapidly after Katrina's 145 mile per hour winds

417
00:30:16,541 --> 00:30:19,140
ripped apart the Superdome's nine acre roof.

418
00:30:20,700 --> 00:30:25,740
Hurricane force winds began to cut, pierce, and slice this roofing system,

419
00:30:26,340 --> 00:30:30,660
resulting in something similar to an onion beginning to peel back the layers.

420
00:30:31,220 --> 00:30:35,560
First the membrane, then the insulation, then the structural deck.

421
00:30:35,561 --> 00:30:43,540
Inside the superdome, rain pouring in from the damaged roof shorted out the main electrical

422
00:30:43,541 --> 00:30:47,320
system. The dome had to depend on a backup generator.

423
00:30:47,321 --> 00:30:55,680
It was like living an epic disaster film. It was an unbelievable experience.

424
00:30:55,681 --> 00:30:56,780
So there's

425
00:30:56,781 --> 00:31:00,980
minimal lighting, there's no air conditioning, and it's just a matter of time before you

426
00:31:00,981 --> 00:31:02,840
lose water pressure.

427
00:31:02,841 --> 00:31:07,360
You have 15 to 20, 25,000 people eventually in your building

428
00:31:07,361 --> 00:31:09,800
using the restroom with no water pressure.

429
00:31:09,801 --> 00:31:11,100
You can imagine what that's like.

430
00:31:11,101 --> 00:31:14,740
So we lost the toilets and the ability for people

431
00:31:14,741 --> 00:31:18,220
to use the facilities in here after a couple of days.

432
00:31:20,840 --> 00:31:22,580
The Superdome was built to withstand

433
00:31:22,581 --> 00:31:25,300
wind stronger than Katrina's gusts.

434
00:31:25,301 --> 00:31:27,500
Yet the structure proved to be another New Orleans

435
00:31:27,501 --> 00:31:28,380
engineering disaster.

436
00:31:30,980 --> 00:31:39,220
Constructed in 1975, the 1.9 million square foot indoor stadium was one of the first sports

437
00:31:39,221 --> 00:31:44,740
domes of its kind.

438
00:31:44,741 --> 00:31:48,320
The stadium itself covers 13 acres.

439
00:31:48,321 --> 00:31:58,740
It reaches 27 stories at its peak and encloses a volume of more than 125 million cubic feet.

440
00:31:58,741 --> 00:32:03,620
20,000 tons of steel and 150,000 cubic yards of concrete

441
00:32:03,621 --> 00:32:05,100
were required for its construction.

442
00:32:07,700 --> 00:32:10,020
And certainly the Superdome was built to the code

443
00:32:10,021 --> 00:32:11,480
at that time in 1975.

444
00:32:12,120 --> 00:32:14,020
The building structurally is very sound.

445
00:32:14,600 --> 00:32:18,520
So yes, it was built to withstand hurricane force winds.

446
00:32:20,320 --> 00:32:22,040
Why then did the roof fail

447
00:32:22,041 --> 00:32:23,640
when subjected to Katrina's winds?

448
00:32:24,340 --> 00:32:26,340
The engineering disaster involved

449
00:32:26,341 --> 00:32:27,880
not the structure's roof itself,

450
00:32:27,881 --> 00:32:30,500
but rather the surface that covers the roof.

451
00:32:31,320 --> 00:32:37,700
It's a rubber membrane over a 2-inch styrofoam-based material

452
00:32:37,701 --> 00:32:39,420
that is laid over a metal deck.

453
00:32:39,840 --> 00:32:44,380
And we lost one of the apex vents, one of the exhaust dampers,

454
00:32:44,740 --> 00:32:48,560
at the very top, very apex of our roof, 270 feet above the floor.

455
00:32:50,280 --> 00:32:54,840
The exhaust dampers are housed in 5-foot-high, 20-foot-long box structures

456
00:32:54,841 --> 00:32:56,600
resting on the roof surface.

457
00:32:57,880 --> 00:33:04,640
When the hurricane winds blew one of the dampers off the roof, the resulting breach exposed the rubberized roof membrane.

458
00:33:05,400 --> 00:33:06,740
It began to tear.

459
00:33:07,700 --> 00:33:13,480
Once this occurred, the winds were able to move under the membrane seal and shred it piece by piece.

460
00:33:14,500 --> 00:33:19,860
The effect on this roof was the removal of almost 70% of the membrane itself,

461
00:33:19,960 --> 00:33:25,660
exposing all of the structural metal deck and opening up the holes into the roof itself.

462
00:33:25,661 --> 00:33:29,260
The super dome roof would have to be rebuilt.

463
00:33:30,840 --> 00:33:36,920
By November 2005, the roof had been covered with a thin, temporary, hardened foam surface.

464
00:33:38,900 --> 00:33:41,720
But now we're going to move to a long-term solution,

465
00:33:41,820 --> 00:33:46,860
a long-term solution of a galvanized structural deck with a new foam roofing system.

466
00:33:47,320 --> 00:33:49,520
Hopefully we'll protect the dome better than ever.

467
00:33:49,800 --> 00:33:54,600
It will result in a better system, a deck that will resist the inevitable next hurricane.

468
00:33:55,660 --> 00:34:03,160
However, in that desperate week after Katrina, there was another engineering disaster, impacting

469
00:34:03,161 --> 00:34:08,940
the thousands in the Superdome and across the city.

470
00:34:08,941 --> 00:34:13,500
The Interstate 10 Twin Span Bridge across Lake Pontchartrain collapsed because of the

471
00:34:13,501 --> 00:34:16,820
hurricane storm surge.

472
00:34:16,821 --> 00:34:20,820
Since this was one of the main routes into the city, relief supplies were difficult to

473
00:34:20,821 --> 00:34:21,820
deliver.

474
00:34:21,821 --> 00:34:30,500
The tremendous force of nature and the awesome power that unleashed on this bridge is something

475
00:34:30,501 --> 00:34:32,000
that you just had to see to believe.

476
00:34:32,260 --> 00:34:38,000
When you're looking at these 265-ton concrete segments just being tossed around like toys.

477
00:34:39,000 --> 00:34:45,120
The Twin Span Bridge was constructed in 1964, linking New Orleans with Interstate 10.

478
00:34:45,121 --> 00:34:51,840
It's a concrete structure spanning 5.4 miles of Lake Pontchartrain.

479
00:34:53,440 --> 00:35:00,460
The bridge consists of approximately 460 concrete segments, each weighing about 265 tons.

480
00:35:02,600 --> 00:35:06,780
The segments are held up by pilings that are sunk 100 feet into the lake bed,

481
00:35:07,380 --> 00:35:09,980
but rise less than 10 feet above the lake's surface.

482
00:35:09,981 --> 00:35:19,760
the pilings withstood the storm surge but because of a design flaw some of the girders supporting

483
00:35:19,761 --> 00:35:27,920
the concrete segments did not under this bridge span right here the tidal surge came up and it

484
00:35:27,921 --> 00:35:34,480
reached probably about to the top of this girder or right around there it trapped air between these

485
00:35:34,481 --> 00:35:41,660
girders, and that caused about a million pounds of pressure that shoved up on this concrete

486
00:35:41,661 --> 00:35:42,361
segment.

487
00:35:42,362 --> 00:35:46,440
At about the same time, you've got probably about three quarters of a million

488
00:35:46,441 --> 00:35:50,761
pounds of pressure from a tidal surge that's hitting the side of this segment.

489
00:35:50,762 --> 00:35:51,462
So all of

490
00:35:51,463 --> 00:35:57,660
that combined to just shove this segment up and out, and it just kind of tossed it into

491
00:35:57,661 --> 00:35:58,740
the lake like it was a domino.

492
00:35:58,741 --> 00:36:06,660
A total of 435 segments of both spans were damaged, making the bridge impassable.

493
00:36:08,840 --> 00:36:13,960
Because this passageway is critical to the city, it needed to be rebuilt as soon as possible.

494
00:36:16,600 --> 00:36:19,960
It took just 17 days to repair one of the spans.

495
00:36:20,960 --> 00:36:28,500
What we had to do was cannibalize segments from one span and put them on the other span.

496
00:36:28,740 --> 00:36:33,560
so that we could establish two-way traffic on one span.

497
00:36:33,561 --> 00:36:37,340
That left us with huge gaps in the remaining span.

498
00:36:38,020 --> 00:36:43,320
And what we did is we used bridge panels that we attached to the current structure,

499
00:36:43,660 --> 00:36:45,960
basically made of steel, galvanized steel.

500
00:36:46,440 --> 00:36:51,520
So instead of replacing those missing spans with other concrete spans,

501
00:36:51,700 --> 00:36:53,120
we put in these bridge panels.

502
00:36:54,300 --> 00:36:58,520
You can see with this new truss system and with these panels,

503
00:36:58,740 --> 00:37:02,620
there's really no place for any significant air pressure to build up.

504
00:37:03,020 --> 00:37:09,880
So it's really not going to be susceptible to the same kind of air pockets and air pressures as the concrete panels were.

505
00:37:10,180 --> 00:37:12,880
So that's one way that you can fix this.

506
00:37:15,220 --> 00:37:22,020
By early January 2006, both sides of the Twin Span Bridge have been rebuilt and opened to traffic.

507
00:37:24,920 --> 00:37:27,320
However, these are just short-term repairs.

508
00:37:28,120 --> 00:37:32,520
The long-term solution to this is to build an elevated bridge

509
00:37:32,521 --> 00:37:34,860
at least twice as high as the current bridge.

510
00:37:35,720 --> 00:37:39,920
We want to make sure that it's not susceptible to that kind of tidal surge.

511
00:37:40,260 --> 00:37:43,580
We believe that kind of bridge would cost about $600 million,

512
00:37:44,180 --> 00:37:46,480
but we believe it would be worth it to ensure

513
00:37:46,481 --> 00:37:48,860
that this type of tragedy never happens again.

514
00:37:50,280 --> 00:37:53,820
The opening of the Twin Span Bridge and the repair of the Superdome

515
00:37:53,821 --> 00:37:57,080
are signs of New Orleans' determination to rise again.

516
00:37:57,320 --> 00:38:06,240
But the question remains, can the city itself, with its miles of devastation, truly be rebuilt?

517
00:38:12,660 --> 00:38:18,640
On Bourbon Street, the neon lights are flashing, and the booze and jazz are flowing.

518
00:38:20,020 --> 00:38:23,040
The Big Easy is making its best effort to come back.

519
00:38:23,860 --> 00:38:27,180
But the French Quarter is located on the city's highest ground.

520
00:38:27,320 --> 00:38:29,720
and received the least amount of flood damage.

521
00:38:31,720 --> 00:38:35,820
Beyond it, vast areas of the city have been extensively damaged.

522
00:38:37,680 --> 00:38:42,580
To fund what is likely to be the largest demolition and rebuilding project in America's history,

523
00:38:43,320 --> 00:38:47,500
the mayor of New Orleans is asking for tens of billions of dollars in federal aid.

524
00:38:49,180 --> 00:38:55,160
We are working very feverishly with banking institutions, with financial folk,

525
00:38:55,161 --> 00:39:01,560
as well as federal officials to secure a line of credit that will sustain us.

526
00:39:02,800 --> 00:39:08,540
But there is a looming, potentially cataclysmic crisis that may doom recovery efforts in New Orleans.

527
00:39:09,360 --> 00:39:14,560
Eventually what's going to happen is that the coast of Louisiana is going to be inundated by the Gulf of Mexico.

528
00:39:18,600 --> 00:39:19,300
Dr.

529
00:39:19,301 --> 00:39:24,460
Roy Daca, a professor of civil and environmental engineering at Louisiana State University,

530
00:39:25,160 --> 00:39:27,960
is now using high-powered global positioning satellites

531
00:39:27,961 --> 00:39:30,900
to develop true elevation points in the state.

532
00:39:31,800 --> 00:39:34,580
Well, this is a GPS measuring device.

533
00:39:34,740 --> 00:39:36,400
This is the antenna up here.

534
00:39:36,720 --> 00:39:38,520
What we're using this for is to figure out

535
00:39:38,521 --> 00:39:40,660
exactly how high we are above sea level,

536
00:39:40,760 --> 00:39:44,080
but more importantly, how low we are below sea level.

537
00:39:44,380 --> 00:39:47,460
And what that's been able to do is that it's shown us

538
00:39:47,461 --> 00:39:50,880
that the sinking is occurring much faster

539
00:39:50,881 --> 00:39:52,340
than what people had thought before,

540
00:39:52,460 --> 00:39:55,040
and it's occurring in places that we didn't know

541
00:39:55,041 --> 00:39:55,741
it was happening.

542
00:39:58,040 --> 00:39:58,740
Dr.

543
00:39:58,741 --> 00:40:03,260
Daca's results indicate the elevations of some areas have dropped as much as two feet

544
00:40:03,261 --> 00:40:05,820
since they were last surveyed in 1970.

545
00:40:06,880 --> 00:40:08,520
And the sinking continues.

546
00:40:10,620 --> 00:40:16,640
According to some estimates, within the next 70 years, about 15,000 square miles of land

547
00:40:16,641 --> 00:40:20,820
in southern Louisiana, known as the wetlands, could be underwater.

548
00:40:20,821 --> 00:40:25,580
The wetlands encompass a marshy region that acts as a buffer zone,

549
00:40:26,200 --> 00:40:28,680
slowing down hurricanes before they reach New Orleans.

550
00:40:30,300 --> 00:40:34,340
We're losing, on average, a football field every hour.

551
00:40:34,940 --> 00:40:38,140
The reason for the sinking is an ecological catch-22,

552
00:40:38,980 --> 00:40:42,180
arising from human efforts to protect against flooding from the Mississippi.

553
00:40:42,860 --> 00:40:45,780
In order to stop the flooding, we levied the rivers.

554
00:40:46,260 --> 00:40:48,560
We kept the river within its bank, we tamed it.

555
00:40:48,561 --> 00:40:52,060
But in the process, we also disrupted the natural system.

556
00:40:52,680 --> 00:40:57,300
Before, the river would naturally fill up areas that had subsided,

557
00:40:57,740 --> 00:41:00,500
and as it filled them up above sea level higher and higher,

558
00:41:00,740 --> 00:41:03,140
the river then would find another course

559
00:41:03,141 --> 00:41:06,420
and begin to fill up other areas that had fallen below sea level.

560
00:41:07,020 --> 00:41:08,260
In addition to the wetlands,

561
00:41:08,880 --> 00:41:11,240
Louisiana's other natural defense against hurricanes

562
00:41:11,241 --> 00:41:12,600
has been the Barrier Islands,

563
00:41:12,860 --> 00:41:15,820
which were part of the region's shoreline just a century ago.

564
00:41:15,821 --> 00:41:21,140
Katrina blasted right through them with Category 5 force.

565
00:41:23,400 --> 00:41:28,780
After Katrina, there's not much left of the barrier islands,

566
00:41:29,140 --> 00:41:33,020
particularly the ones that have been protecting New Orleans.

567
00:41:33,320 --> 00:41:35,200
Much of them has been washed away.

568
00:41:35,201 --> 00:41:39,300
When the barrier islands become diminished or disappear,

569
00:41:39,760 --> 00:41:45,400
then there's not that impedance there to slow the action of the hurricane down.

570
00:41:45,820 --> 00:41:52,000
Perhaps it was a fundamental design disaster to build the city of New Orleans on this land.

571
00:41:53,380 --> 00:41:56,280
Authorities are faced with an increasingly complex decision,

572
00:41:57,000 --> 00:42:02,000
whether to invest enormous amounts of time and money to attempt to completely protect the city

573
00:42:02,001 --> 00:42:07,780
with a newly designed and engineered levee system, or to simply patch and rebuild the existing system.

574
00:42:08,380 --> 00:42:12,880
This is not just a New Orleans problem, but it's an entire coastal problem.

575
00:42:12,881 --> 00:42:18,480
And that means what we need to do is to build some sort of structure that's going to keep the water out,

576
00:42:18,820 --> 00:42:21,560
building a levee that might span the entire coast.

577
00:42:21,920 --> 00:42:25,560
This would be the biggest engineering project in the history of the earth.

578
00:42:25,700 --> 00:42:28,960
This would make the pyramids look like nothing.

579
00:42:29,740 --> 00:42:33,260
And so what we need to do is to start new ways of thinking,

580
00:42:33,500 --> 00:42:37,580
start thinking about building the Great Wall of Louisiana.

581
00:42:37,581 --> 00:42:44,580
But whatever the decision, the outcome for New Orleans may

582
00:42:44,581 --> 00:42:48,580
ultimately lie in the unpredictable hands of Mother

583
00:42:48,581 --> 00:42:53,580
Nature and her power to transform the Big Easy into a

584
00:42:53,581 --> 00:42:56,580
modern-day Atlantis.

